  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe summer swelter continues through this weekend with above\nnormal temperatures and dry conditions. Hot and near record\ntemperatures continue into the first half of next week, but the\noverall conditions become a little more favorable for streamer\nshowers in the morning and 20 percent chance for afternoon sea-breeze\nthunderstorms.\n\nThe big talking point for next week is the potential arrival of a\n\"cold\" front late Wednesday into Thursday. However, there is\nsignificant uncertainty regarding the southern extent of the\nfront, the strength, and the timing.\n\nComplicating the situation is Hurricane Polo and how it will\ninteract with the parent trough as it moves across the Desert\nSouthwest early next week. There are a couple different possible\nscenarios, the tropical system could help to deepen the trough,\nwhich would make it more likely to track farther south and then\nbring rain to the region, or it may not have much impact on the\ndeepening of the trough, keeping it farther north, and thus a\nbetter chance of the front washing out before it reaches South\nTexas.\n\nRegardless, it does look like we will see increased chances for\nrain later next week and some slightly cooler temperatures.",
